---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { srcLink, sourceList } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { FETCHED, LATEST, FIRST, exName, day, EXERCISES } from '../../lib/data';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'COE Cost 2026: Publisher, Data Sources and Independence';
const description = 'Who runs COE Cost in 2026: Radif Partners, COE results from data.gov.sg after each exercise, LTA tax rules rewritten as calculators, no dealer, no lead form.';
const s = (k: string, t: string) => srcLink(k, lang, t);
---
<PageLayout sources={sourceList(['dgs-coe', 'dgs-pqp', 'sodl', 'lta-tax'], lang)} title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>About COE Cost</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why the site was built</h2>
    <p>In Singapore the sticker on a new car is the sum of six or seven separate charges, and the money you get back when the car goes to the scrapyard depends on two more rules. COE results are easy to find, but they are usually shown as a bare list of premiums. Calculators exist, but most of them handle one tax at a time, so a buyer has to copy a figure from one page to the next and still has no idea of the yearly cost. Some pages that show the results are there to collect a phone number for a car sale, a loan or an insurance quote.</p>
    <p><strong>This site chains the whole calculation in one tool: OMV, excise duty and GST, tiered ARF, VES and EEAI, the COE premium of the latest exercise, road tax, then the PARF and COE rebates at the end, so that the answer is a cost per year and per month. The COE figures are refreshed from the official open data after each exercise, and nothing asks for your name or number.</strong></p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds calculators from public data. It is not the Land Transport Authority, not a car dealer, not a bank and not an insurer. It sells no car, loan or policy, takes no commission, and its pages carry no affiliate link and no lead form.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>Where the figures come from</h2>
    <p>COE premiums, quotas and bids for all {EXERCISES.length} exercises from {exName(FIRST, lang)} to {exName(LATEST, lang)} come from the Land Transport Authority’s data set on <Fragment set:html={s('dgs-coe', 'data.gov.sg')} />, and the monthly prevailing quota premium from the Singapore Department of Statistics data set (source: LTA) on <Fragment set:html={s('dgs-pqp', 'data.gov.sg')} />, both read on {day(FETCHED, lang)} and reused under the <Fragment set:html={s('sodl', 'Singapore Open Data Licence version 1.0')} />. The tax rules (ARF tiers, VES bands, EEAI caps, road tax formulas, PARF schedules, COE rebate and renewal rules) were read on LTA’s <Fragment set:html={s('lta-tax', 'vehicle tax structure page')} /> and the pages cited on each calculator page, then written into a parameter file that the calculators read. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> lists every rule and its source; the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains how corrections are made.</p>
    <h2>What the site does not do</h2>
    <p>It cannot see your vehicle record, tell you the OMV of a particular car, place a bid or renew a COE. Its results are estimates built from the published rules: the amounts on LTA’s own enquiry services, your dealer’s contract and your bank’s letter are the ones that count.</p>
    <h2>Write to us</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, to report a figure that differs from LTA’s, a rule that changed or a calculation that looks wrong.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
